              evaluation: `This is Tier B at its best. Sacks was a working clinical neurologist (Albert Einstein College of Medicine, NYU, Columbia); the book engages scholarly research throughout; case studies are clinically substantial; the prose is accessible. What it does well: makes complex research engageable for non-specialists; provides concrete clinical examples that make abstract findings vivid; respects the scientific rigor while writing for general readers; suggests therapeutic applications grounded in actual clinical experience. Limits worth noting: case studies are not the same epistemic level as controlled studies (individual cases can be misleading even when accurate); Sacks occasionally extrapolates beyond what specific cases support; some claims in the book have been subsequently refined or revised by further research. For caring for someone with dementia: yes, Musicophilia is a useful starting point, but it should be paired with consultation with the patient's actual care team about specific music therapy approaches. How to read Tier B: as accessible bridge to Tier A research; verify specific findings in original papers when stakes matter; recognize that even careful popular books simplify.`,
