              evaluation: `This is Tier C-pseudoscience. What empirical claims are being made: specific frequencies have specific physical-mechanism effects on biology (DNA repair) and consciousness (universal harmonic alignment); modern 440 Hz tuning has specific negative effects; 528 Hz has specific positive effects through specific physical mechanism. What's testable: all of these are testable claims. What does testing show: no peer-reviewed research supports the specific claims; the "DNA repair" claim has no biological basis (DNA repair mechanisms are well-studied and don't involve specific audio frequencies); the "natural frequency" claim is asserted but not demonstrated; controlled studies don't find the claimed effects. What's the actual basis: Joseph Puleo's 1990s Bible numerology derivation, which isn't science; subsequent amplification through wellness culture, which doesn't constitute evidence. Why engaging this accurately matters: friends and family who encounter this content (substantial reach on YouTube and wellness platforms) may make purchasing decisions or health decisions based on the claims; if music genuinely helps someone with mood or relaxation, that benefit is real (placebo and intentional engagement produce real effects), but the specific mechanism claims are not supported. Engaging accurately means being able to say "music that helps you relax helps you relax; the specific 528 Hz claims aren't supported; you don't need them to be true for music to genuinely help."`,
