              context: `Sufi qawwali is the devotional music tradition of Sufi Islam, practiced primarily in Pakistan and India, intended to induce mystical states (haal, wajd) in performers and prepared listeners. Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan (1948-1997) was its most internationally famous practitioner, performing globally and substantially expanding its recognition. Documentaries on the tradition typically combine: practitioner interviews engaging the tradition's framework; performance footage; scholarly commentary (often from ethnomusicologists or Sufi scholars); contemporary musicians engaging the tradition. The documentaries vary in quality and rigor.`,
              quote: `"[Practitioner interview] When the qawwali is at its peak, the boundary dissolves. The singer is no longer singing; the music sings through the singer. Listeners enter wajd, the state of ecstatic remembrance. [Ethnomusicologist commentary] What we're seeing here has been documented across the tradition for centuries. The neurological mechanisms involve repetitive rhythm, melodic phrases that create expectation and resolution, sustained tones that engage altered states of attention, group dynamics that produce social-bonding neurochemistry. The tradition's claims about transcendence are not testable in physics terms, but the practice's effects on practitioners and prepared listeners are real."`,
